WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.740
A Node.js service in Kubernetes is not deployed once and forgotten.

2
00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:11.720
It is changed, restarted, scaled, rolled back, debugged, and investigated over time.

3
00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:18.780
Deployments support rolling updates, replica management, and rollback behavior, but rollout safety depends on the rest of the design.

4
00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:24.720
If readiness probes are accurate, Kubernetes can avoid routing traffic to Pods that are not prepared.

5
00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:30.180
If resource settings are realistic, new replicas have a better chance of surviving production traffic.

6
00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:37.000
If logs and metrics are useful, operators can understand whether a rollout is improving or degrading the service.

7
00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:39.600
Scaling should be connected to real bottlenecks.

8
00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:50.900
Horizontal scaling can help when more replicas improve throughput, but it does not fix inefficient code, missing resource limits, slow dependencies, database saturation, queue backlogs, or event-loop blocking.

9
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:59.640
Autoscaling signals might include CPU, memory, request rate, latency, queue depth, or custom business metrics, depending on the workload.

10
00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:07.660
The key is to scale based on signals that reflect service demand and user impact, not simply because autoscaling is available.

11
00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:12.020
Observability should start with the operational questions the team needs to answer.

12
00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:16.440
Logs should go to stdout and stderr in a structured, searchable form.

13
00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:24.220
Metrics should cover request rate, error rate, latency, memory, CPU, restarts, dependency failures, and queue behavior.

14
00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:30.400
For Node.js, event-loop symptoms and runtime memory trends can be especially useful.

15
00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:35.540
Tracing can show how requests move through multiple services and where time is spent.

16
00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:42.280
Alerts should focus on user impact and actionable conditions rather than every noisy internal change.

17
00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:45.840
Runbooks turn repeated failures into a support workflow.

18
00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:58.740
A useful runbook explains how to investigate CrashLoopBackOff, ImagePullBackOff, readiness failures, OOMKilled containers, bad rollouts, configuration errors, and unexpected traffic drops.

19
00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:08.120
It points operators to Pod status, events, logs, metrics, deployment history, recent changes, and rollback procedures.

20
00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:11.180
Mature Kubernetes operation is not just writing YAML.

21
00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:20.640
It is connecting manifests, CI/CD, deployment events, telemetry, and incident response into a system the team can reason about under pressure.
